00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.080
Overnight shipping is an absolute masterpiece
of logistics that happens every single

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.080
night.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:19.350
It may not be cheap, but you can get a package
shipped from Miami, Florida on a Monday

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.950
night to Anchorage, Alaska, by 8:30 AM on
Tuesday.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:28.100
In fact, you can even ship a package, for
example, from Edinburgh, Scotland on a Tuesday

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.520
and have the package arrive in Anchorage,
Alaska by 9am on Wednesday.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.220
The speed and efficiency of these worldwide
delivery networks is

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:39.230
mind-blowing and it all happens while we sleep.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:45.230
The three major consumer courier companies
are FedEx, DHL, and UPS and each is as

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:46.870
impressive as the last.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:50.240
FedEx has more planes than Emirates, Etihad,
and Qatar Airways

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.890
combined; DHL delivers to every country in
the world including North Korea; and UPS flies

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.280
to
more than double as many destinations as the

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.160
largest passenger airline.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.420
Each has a global
network that allows for lightning fast shipping

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.420
at relatively low prices.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.440
Behind all this speed are
enormous air networks that connect the entire

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.070
world daily.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:15.070
Each of these three operates hundreds
of flights nightly, but FedEx is the best

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.090
example since their operations make them the
largest

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.060
cargo airline in the world.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:24.801
They have 650 planes flying to 400 destinations
carrying 6 million

00:01:24.801 --> 00:01:30.260
packages every single day and the vast majority
of these flights operate to or from one of

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.750
their
hub airports.

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:36.340
FedEx’s hub airports are spread out all
across the world and serve as sorting points

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.690
where
packages are transferred from one plane to

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:39.690
another.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:44.720
They has hubs in Singapore, Guangzhou,
Shanghai, Seoul, Osaka, Anchorage, Oakland,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:51.260
Dallas, Indianapolis, Greensboro, Miami, Newark,
Toronto, Paris, Cologne, Milan, and Dubai,

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.990
but the most important hub of all is the one
in

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.640
Memphis, Tennessee because that’s their
SuperHub.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:03.170
Memphis is not a huge city—only about 650,000
people live there—but the reason FedEx

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:07.950
centers their worldwide operations in this
city is because of it’s location.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.599
Memphis is not actually
in the geographic center of the US as might

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:13.659
make sense, but it is central.

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:17.930
You see, only about 200
miles away in Wright County, Missouri is the

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.790
mean population center of the US.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.689
This is the
average location of every resident in the

00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:26.379
US meaning that the FedEx SuperHub in Memphis
is

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:30.760
the best location to reach the most people
in the shortest amount of time.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.060
For similar reasons,
UPS has their equivalent global hub, Worldport,

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:36.659
nearby in Louisville, Kentucky.

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:40.680
The scale of
FedEx and UPS’ operations in these relatively

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.010
small cities is staggering.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:46.230
This is the size of the
commercial terminal at Memphis Airport while

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.209
this is the size of FedEx’s Superhub.

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:51.519
The
difference at Louisville airport is even more

00:02:51.519 --> 00:02:56.760
pronounced where this is the commercial terminal
and this is UPS’ worldport.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.499
You can’t even fly to the west coast non-stop
on a commercial airline

00:03:00.499 --> 00:03:06.579
from Louisville and yet UPS flies from this
small city to five different continents.

00:03:06.579 --> 00:03:09.730
FedEx’s
operations in Memphis, meanwhile, make this

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.569
airport the second busiest cargo airport in
the

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:18.379
world above those of enormous cities like
Tokyo, Paris, Dubai, Shanghai, and falling

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:20.370
short only
to Hong Kong.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:25.839
How the FedEx superhub really works is that
every night, about 150 planes fly in from

00:03:25.839 --> 00:03:28.459
all
around the world between the hours of 10pm

00:03:28.459 --> 00:03:29.569
and 1am.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:33.819
Immediately upon arrival, the planes are
unloaded and their packages are put into the

00:03:33.819 --> 00:03:35.680
hub’s automated sorting system.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.719
Within only 15
minutes, each package arrives at a staging

00:03:38.719 --> 00:03:41.390
area for its next flight where it’s loaded
into

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:42.390
containers.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:47.239
Planes therefore can start taking off again
at 2am and continue to until 4am which

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:52.559
means that everywhere in the US can have a
FedEx plane arriving by 6am, but there are

00:03:52.559 --> 00:03:54.760
some
destinations that don’t ship enough packages

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.450
to need a non-stop flight to Memphis.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.900
To get to
small towns fast, FedEx runs flights in small

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.529
propeller aircraft from the destinations of
their

00:04:03.529 --> 00:04:04.879
larger jets.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:09.459
Presque Isle, Maine, for example, is far too
small of a town at about 10,000 residents

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:14.719
to fill a full-size plane so, every morning,
once the larger planes from Memphis arrive

00:04:14.719 --> 00:04:17.340
in
Manchester, New Hampshire and Portland, Maine,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:22.440
packages bound for Presque Isle are sorted
into smaller prop planes that continue north.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.690
With this system, even small towns like Presque
Isle

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:31.470
get their packages by 9am as every spoke in
the system essentially functions as a mini-hub.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:36.190
Packages are transferred from planes, to smaller
planes, to trucks to reach their destination

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:37.760
as fast
as possible.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.000
Now, it’s important to note that not every
FedEx package runs through Memphis.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.310
That
would be incredibly inefficient if a customer

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:50.250
wanted to, for example, ship a package from
Phoenix, Arizona to Seattle, Washington.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.190
While only 1,100 miles separate Seattle from
Phoenix,

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:58.290
a routing through Memphis would total over
3,000 miles and six hours in flight.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.980
The package
would still make it overnight, but FedEx would

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:07.340
be wasting fuel carrying that package an extra
1,900 miles, so that’s why they have secondary

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.340
hubs.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:12.410
In this case, FedEx’s Oakland hub has
flights to both Phoenix and Seattle so the

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.930
package would take a relatively efficient
1,300 mile

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:16.930
routing.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:21.090
Memphis essentially serves as the backup hub
in case there’s not a more efficient

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.090
routing.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.690
The secondary hubs, such as Oakland, in general
have flights to destinations that are

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.210
already served by flights to Memphis, but
the destinations from Oakland are high demand

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:35.120
destinations that will ship enough packages
solely to the west coast to fill entire planes

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.310
to
Oakland.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.460
Some destinations, such as Albuquerque, New
Mexico, ship enough packages to fill

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.990
entire planes to Memphis, but not enough to
fill flights to Oakland with west coast bound

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:49.520
packages so a package shipped from here to
the west coast would likely take a rather

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.500
inefficient
routing backtracking to Memphis.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:57.020
But FedEx’s most ingenious hub is here in
Anchorage, Alaska.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:01.420
Anchorage, with fewer
than 300,000 residents, is home to the forth

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:04.300
busiest cargo airport in the world.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.520
This is, once
again, thanks to geography.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.680
If you draw a straight line from FedEx’s
Memphis hub to the one in

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:16.610
Osaka, taking into account earth’s curvature,
it goes directly over Anchorage, Alaska.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.210
This
airport is just the perfect stop-over point

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:21.470
for flights from the US to Asia.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:24.750
Now, dozens of cargo
airlines operate in Anchorage but most of

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.860
them just use the airport as a refueling and
crew swap

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:28.860
spot.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:33.000
Modern airplanes can fly non-stop from the
contiguous United States to Asia, but doing

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.110
so
requires taking more fuel which requires taking

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.110
less cargo.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:41.980
It’s just cheaper to stop in Anchorage,
but FedEx and UPS use the stop for something

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:43.720
else—sorting.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:47.060
If FedEx wanted to maintain
current shipping times without the Anchorage

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.310
hub, they would likely have to run non-stop
flights

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:53.060
from each of their Asian hubs to each of their
American hubs, but they just don’t have

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.460
the
demand to fill this many planes.

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.520
Instead, they run flights from their Asian
hubs to Anchorage

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.460
then flights from Anchorage many of their
American hubs.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:05.050
While stopped in Anchorage,
packages from Asia are processed through customs

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:08.990
and sorted to be put on the plane bound
closest to their destination.

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:12.740
This helps cuts down on shipping time and
cost.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.930
Shipping is an incredibly price-sensitive
business.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.991
These courier companies rely on
enormous contracts with retailers and, when

00:07:19.991 --> 00:07:22.240
some of these retailers are shipping millions
of

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.590
packages per day, every cent matters.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:28.360
In a lot of ways, however, the express shipping
model is

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:35.650
inherently expensive largely because of how
couriers use their most expensive assets—planes.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:40.360
So much is centered around those few sorting
hours at the big hubs so FedEx’s planes

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.460
all have to
wait around to arrive at the exact right moment.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:49.540
Some FedEx hubs, such as Memphis, do sort
packages during the day, but the overwhelming

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:52.380
majority of their business happens overnight.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:57.430
FedEx’s flight from Memphis to Oklahoma
City, for example, leaves at 4am and arrives

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:00.800
at
5:20am, but then the plane waits around until

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.260
10:10pm to fly back to Memphis.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.770
That’s over 17
hours sitting in Oklahoma City and, on that

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.770
route, the plane is only flying for about
two hours per

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:12.770
day.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.730
Meanwhile, commercial airlines regularly fly
their planes for more than 12 hours per day

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:21.610
meaning they have six times higher aircraft
utilization.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:26.020
FedEx would never be profitable if they
bought all new multi-hundred million dollar

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:29.950
aircraft to use for mere hours per day, so
they don’t.

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:35.159
Overwhelmingly, FedEx and other cargo airlines
use old aircraft at the end of their lives.

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:38.159
You’ll
almost never see Airbus a300’s flying for

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:42.339
passenger airlines anymore, yet FedEx, UPS,
and DHL

00:08:42.339 --> 00:08:45.809
collectively own hundreds of them because
they’re cheap.

00:08:45.809 --> 00:08:49.199
They didn’t spend much purchasing
these aircraft, so they don’t have to worry

00:08:49.199 --> 00:08:52.170
about using them enough to offset their cost.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.189
UPS does
have some brand new 747-800 aircraft, which

00:08:56.189 --> 00:08:58.749
are highly efficient, but they specifically
schedule

00:08:58.749 --> 00:09:03.360
these planes on their longest routes so that
they can recuperate their high purchase price

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.160
through
lower fuel costs.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.449
With older aircraft, fuel costs might be higher
since the planes are less efficient,

00:09:09.449 --> 00:09:13.959
but overall it’s worth it since it allows
FedEx to profitably leave their planes sitting

00:09:13.959 --> 00:09:16.370
for all but a
few hours each day.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:20.240
Some passenger airlines, such as Allegiant
Airlines in the US, uses the same

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:25.350
strategy purchasing cheaper planes to allow
them to fly fewer hours per day profitably

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:28.670
and its
now a tested and proven business strategy.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:32.519
Express shipping is one of those businesses
that requires enormous networks to make

00:09:32.519 --> 00:09:35.680
work which is why you don’t see small shipping
companies.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.589
It’s almost impossible to get started
in this business unless, of course, you can

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:42.339
make your own demand.

00:09:42.339 --> 00:09:46.029
Amazon, which ships more
than a million packages per day, is getting

00:09:46.029 --> 00:09:47.370
into the delivery business.

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:51.499
They’ve established a fleet
of 32 aircraft and are building out their

00:09:51.499 --> 00:09:52.899
logistics network.

00:09:52.899 --> 00:09:57.040
When shipping so many packages,
Amazon is operating at a scale where they

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.009
can profit by taking the shipping companies
out of the

00:10:00.009 --> 00:10:01.009
equation.

00:10:01.009 --> 00:10:06.079
FedEx, UPS, and DHL, meanwhile, are continuously
focusing on further increasing the

00:10:06.079 --> 00:10:11.709
efficiency of their networks since in this
business more than any, time is money.

